<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michel Aflaq</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1910">1910</a>–<a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michel Aflaq was a Syrian philosopher, sociologist and Arab nationalist, considered by several Ba'athists to be the principal founder of Ba'athist thought. Born into a middle-class <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christian family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, he studied at the Sorbonne, and in 1940 founded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Salah al-Din</a> al-Bitar the Arab Ihya Movement, which merged with Zaki al-Arsuzi's group in 1947 to form the Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baath-party">Ba'ath Party</a>. His Ba'athism held that the Arab world must be unified into one Arab Nation, and joined Arab socialism to secularism. Ousted in the 1966 Syrian coup d'état, he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, where he was Secretary General of the Iraqi-led Ba'ath Party from 1968 until his death in 1989, without de facto power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michel-aflaq.jpg" alt="Michel Aflaq" width="330" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Basch, Fritz · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michel_Aflaq_1963.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
